  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Pleasant, uneventful early Fall weather to persist into the\nfirst part of the weekend.\n\nToday should be breezy and seasonably cool behind the \ncold front that moved through overnight. Wind gusts will reach\nin the 20 to 30 mph range, with the stronger gusts in the higher\nterrain. Highs should range from the lower-to-mid 60s in the \nhigher terrain, to the low 70s elsewhere.\n\nFriday and Saturday should remain dry, as a narrow ridge of\nsurface high pressure translates across the Commonwealth. By\nSaturday, temperatures should begin to rebound, as low-level \nwarm advection ensues. Highs Saturday afternoon should range \nfrom the upper 60s over the higher terrain, to the mid-upper 70s\nin the Susquehanna Valley.\n\n-------------------------------------------------------------\n \nKEY MESSAGE 2: Rain chances increase Sunday into Monday, as the remnants of\nIsaias quickly move through the Commonwealth.\n\nOur ensemble prediction systems remain fairly consistent in\nshowing a quick eastward movement of Isaias' remnants and\nassociated moisture from the OH Valley across the Mid-Atlantic\nfrom later this weekend into early Monday. From this early\nvantage point, it looks like the most persistent/concentrated\nrainfall across Central PA will be centered on the Sunday\nafternoon to Sunday night period. Initial indications are that a\ncombination of a short time window for heavier rainfall (most\nlikely 12-18 hours, perhaps even less), plus a welcome recent\nstretch of drier conditions, should significantly mitigate the\npotential for sustained heavy rainfall/flooding. That being\nsaid, we'll continue to watch the progress of this system\nclosely over the coming days. \n\nTuesday morning into Tuesday night, models show a stronger cold\nfront moving across the state, bringing more widespread rain \nchances. Gusty winds and cooler temperatures will follow on \nWednesday.",
